{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"src/cli/commands/emit.rs","issuer":"mailto:claude-review@anthropic.com","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-05-06T21:03:14.919264Z","id":"d7b8f76adcbb216d79c38355947ea09f8936f77b8c71601d5b88382b98454962","body":{"detail":"When 'qualifier emit <custom-type> <subject> --body ...' is invoked with a record_type other than annotation/epoch/dependency, build_record() builds an envelope with id:\"\" (line 150) and round-trips through Record. The deserializer routes custom types to Record::Unknown(value), and finalize_record's Unknown arm is a passthrough (annotation.rs:831 'other => other'), so the empty id is never replaced. Reproduced: 'qualifier emit https://example.com/custom/v1 test.rs --body {\"foo\":\"bar\"}' wrote a line with \"id\":\"\". Such records are not addressable by id-prefix and break content-addressing.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":130},"end":{"line":160},"content_hash":"e578dcb79e1d71479ff8f865f5cb9715e5d8a838bb2e5a5957a70a9997a08087"},"suggested_fix":"Compute a BLAKE3 id for Unknown records too — either by adding an Unknown arm to finalize_record (hashing the canonical-ordered envelope serialization with id=\"\"), or by computing the id directly in build_record before the from_value round-trip.","summary":"emit with custom record types produces records with empty 'id'","tags":["review","bug"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"src/cli/commands/emit.rs","issuer":"mailto:claude-review@anthropic.com","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-05-06T21:03:25.068400Z","id":"12542525d52f83be6475df6bc3d6fb7e2aca3740a603987f1ba741fe2e4003c5","body":{"detail":"build_record() for non-annotation types constructs a serde_json::Map (BTreeMap by default; serde_json is built without the preserve_order feature) and inserts envelope fields in canonical order, but BTreeMap reorders them alphabetically on serialization. Reproduced output: '{\"body\":...,\"created_at\":...,\"id\":\"\",\"issuer\":...,\"metabox\":\"1\",\"subject\":...,\"type\":...}' — alphabetical, not the spec's metabox/type/subject/issuer/issuer_type/created_at/id/body order. By contrast, the typed Annotation/Epoch/Dependency arms serialize correctly because struct field order is preserved by serde derive. This violates SPEC §canonical-form invariants and makes Unknown-record IDs (once the empty-id bug is fixed) inconsistent with typed-record IDs.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":135},"end":{"line":153},"content_hash":"b5197b71132a5750988461104da0c346c8190e102715e7c413df4e0cb0e217a4"},"suggested_fix":"Either enable serde_json's 'preserve_order' feature (Cargo.toml) so Map insertion order is preserved, or build the JSON via a typed wrapper struct with the correct field order, mirroring AnnotationCanonicalView.","summary":"emit's Unknown records serialize in alphabetical key order, not canonical Metabox envelope order","tags":["review","bug","canonical-form"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"src/cli/commands/freshness.rs","issuer":"mailto:claude-review@anthropic.com","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-05-06T21:03:32.428514Z","id":"65a84bf71d2265d5e185340d4643c8ec8c3e93f51308cdb5d4c2a3beb706512a","body":{"detail":"freshness::run() sets 'let root = Path::new(\".\")' and discovers from there. Other commands (show, ls, praise, reply, resolve, compact --all) call qual_file::find_project_root(Path::new(\".\")) first and only fall back to '.' if no VCS marker is found. Reproduced: from a subdir of a project that has annotations under .qual at the root, 'qualifier review' prints 'No .qual files found.' while every other command sees the records. The same flaw also means span freshness checks resolve subject paths relative to CWD rather than the project root.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":32},"end":{"line":32},"content_hash":"cc8a38222603f710e47857d33fe01e5f7a8719fa785bc2c50253041e1e72a8dd"},"suggested_fix":"Mirror show.rs / ls.rs: 'let root = qual_file::find_project_root(Path::new(\".\")); let discover_root = root.as_deref().unwrap_or(Path::new(\".\"));' and pass discover_root to discover(). Then use discover_root (or the resolved project root) when interpreting subject as a path for check_freshness.","summary":"qualifier review walks from CWD instead of the project root, so it finds nothing when run from a subdirectory","tags":["review","bug"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"src/cli/commands/ls.rs","issuer":"mailto:claude-review@anthropic.com","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-05-06T21:03:41.980160Z","id":"da1fabb901f1ff8a287213848ab2415caefb01ac1b65c936b821fa07f665a6ef","body":{"detail":"Args::unqualified has the help text 'Show only unannotated artifacts (no annotations)' but the implementation comments 'we can't fully list \"what doesn't exist\"; approximate by listing nothing. The flag stays as a placeholder.' and produces an empty Vec. Users who pass --unqualified see 'No matching artifacts found.' (or '(unqualified listing requires a project file index — not implemented)' depending on path) with no indication that the flag is non-functional. This is a footgun.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":46},"end":{"line":60},"content_hash":"e04a25a83b943bab5118494b489b3f410a4bef03e7b7ae0a8cff6cb9e6f8c082"},"suggested_fix":"Either remove the flag and return an error suggesting an alternative, or implement it: walk the project tree (already needed for discover) and list files that have no matching subject in by_subject. The walk could reuse the ignore::Walk with a filter for source-like extensions, or accept an explicit --include glob.","summary":"ls --unqualified is a documented stub that always returns empty","tags":["review","bug","ux"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"src/cli/commands/ls.rs","issuer":"mailto:claude-review@anthropic.com","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-05-06T21:03:49.490119Z","id":"2ee5791b02b2ca3aa1647983ac4c19645065f6a4124edf9fc5a1be35893ab1c4","body":{"detail":"Two related issues in the row-building code: (1) by_subject is populated from ALL records — superseded ones are not filtered out via compact::filter_superseded — so a subject whose only concern was resolved still shows up with that concern's kind in its kinds vector, and the displayed annotation count includes the historical record. (2) When --kind is passed, the filter selects subjects with ANY kind matching, but '({n} annotations)' prints kinds.len() (total), not the number of records of the requested kind. Example: a file with 3 annotations of which 1 is a 'concern' will print '(3 annotations)' under 'qualifier ls --kind concern', misleading the user about how many concerns exist.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":33},"end":{"line":60},"content_hash":"f9f6b082a63495dbcb0ca45ed1fd7010f6021fd0d075403c72f78e45d5be4e37"},"suggested_fix":"Run filter_superseded over the discovered records before grouping, and when --kind is set, count only records whose kind matches the filter (or display both totals). Optionally also exclude resolve-kind tombstones unless --all is added (mirroring show.rs).","summary":"ls counts include superseded records and conflate kinds when --kind is set","tags":["review","bug","ux"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"src/cli/commands/record.rs","issuer":"mailto:claude-review@anthropic.com","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-05-06T21:04:31.366792Z","id":"b5b9f7b2f284c6b9337232eb07844ceb19d16531fa7dbaf308ca7901db003143","body":{"detail":"Each of record.rs, reply.rs, resolve.rs, and emit.rs reproduces the same blocks: (1) 'normalize_issuer_uri(args.issuer.or_else(detect_issuer).unwrap_or_else(|| \"mailto:unknown@localhost\".into()))' — and the .unwrap_or_else fallback is dead code because detect_issuer's chain ends with Some(format!(\"mailto:{user}@localhost\")) and never returns None; (2) 'match args.issuer_type { Some(s) => s.parse::<IssuerType>().map_err(...), None => None }'; (3) parse-existing-file + check_supersession_cycles + validate_supersession_targets when supersedes is set. Drift risk and tedious changes.","kind":"suggestion","span":{"start":{"line":110},"end":{"line":119},"content_hash":"18ed8dfd9ee052067dada024dd819c48b12fe0e8c66dad08fcdaddbbac595d71"},"suggested_fix":"Extract a shared helper, e.g. mod cli::common with build_issuer(args_issuer: Option<&str>, args_issuer_type: Option<&str>) -> Result<(String, Option<IssuerType>)> and verify_supersession(qual_path: &Path, candidate: &Record) -> Result<()>. Each command then calls into the helper. Bonus: fix the dead 'mailto:unknown@localhost' fallback while consolidating.","summary":"issuer URI/issuer-type plumbing and supersession pre-flight are duplicated across record/reply/resolve/emit","tags":["review","refactor","duplication"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"src/cli/commands/show.rs","issuer":"mailto:claude-review@anthropic.com","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-05-06T21:04:59.688554Z","id":"d02772f915399edb55722a27d5a55ff6a62d4ea4ce6176cb621465edc3f391e6","body":{"detail":"show.rs treats 'no records found' as Err(Validation), causing a non-zero exit and an error printed to stderr. ls.rs for the same case prints 'No matching artifacts found.' on stdout and exits 0. Scripts piping show into other tools have to either suppress stderr or whitelist that specific message; a programmatic consumer can't distinguish 'no annotations' from 'invalid invocation' by exit code alone. The praise command also returns Err on empty, matching show but still inconsistent with ls.","kind":"suggestion","span":{"start":{"line":50},"end":{"line":50},"content_hash":"af1349b9f5f9a1a6a0404dea36dcc9499bcb25c9adc112b7cc9a93cae41f3262"},"suggested_fix":"Standardize: 'no records' is informational, not an error. Print a friendly message on stdout (or empty JSON [] for --format json) and exit 0 in show/praise too. Reserve non-zero exit for actual errors (invalid kind, malformed args, IO failure). If a script wants to error on empty, it can pipe through 'test -s' or check JSON length.","summary":"show returns exit code 1 when no records exist for a subject, but ls returns 0 — pick one convention","tags":["review","ux","exit-code"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"src/cli/commands/diff.rs","issuer":"mailto:claude-review@anthropic.com","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-05-07T18:24:21.788558Z","id":"3d50a1caf30fa746a15f2002c10be5b1ee4c8e8675a2f9acca77c8ebf54d3c53","body":{"detail":"term_width() at line 671-677 reads std::env::var(\"COLUMNS\"). $COLUMNS is a shell variable, not exported into the environment by default in bash/zsh. Most users running 'qualifier diff' from an interactive shell will hit the .unwrap_or(80) fallback even on a 200-column terminal, defeating the 'wrap to terminal width' premise the module-level doc and 3e6cdeb commit message imply. The doc-comment 'set by most shells when stdout is a TTY' is misleading — set, yes; exported, no.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":671},"end":{"line":677},"content_hash":"f1c6fd525c580c75d01a2b6aa07ba62527fef6b3828c3c1dd25f010aa5bbdbdd"},"suggested_fix":"Use the terminal_size crate (already common, ~150 LOC, no transitive cost) or call libc::ioctl(STDOUT_FILENO, TIOCGWINSZ, ...) directly. Keep $COLUMNS as a manual override above the ioctl result for predictable testing.","summary":"term_width() reads only $COLUMNS, which most shells don't export — wrapping rarely adapts to the real terminal","tags":["review","bug"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"src/cli/commands/record.rs","issuer":"mailto:claude-review@anthropic.com","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-05-07T18:24:31.108520Z","id":"8e9e728f37b4f84b00c22112318f8154e4fa5a452b770990250d468e63b71f08","body":{"detail":"Args.file (line 65: 'Explicit .qual file to write to (overrides layout resolution)') is read in run() at line 146 but never threaded into run_batch()/process_one(). Inside process_one (line 327), qual_file::resolve_qual_path(record.subject(), None) hard-codes None, so 'qualifier record --stdin --file foo.qual <feed' writes wherever layout resolution lands — usually a different file. No error, no warning. Confusing for the documented use case 'feed many records into one file'.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":308},"end":{"line":348},"content_hash":"786285ab278562a65c628f3e2b039ee393fdcbe062fb2cd8b46decce06acc7c3"},"suggested_fix":"Plumb the --file value through run_batch into process_one and pass it as the second arg of resolve_qual_path. Or, if intentional, document under --stdin that --file is ignored and reject the combination at clap parse time.","summary":"process_one ignores --file in stdin batch mode, silently dropping the documented override","tags":["review","bug"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"src/cli/commands/record.rs","issuer":"mailto:claude-review@anthropic.com","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-05-07T18:24:37.457424Z","id":"0c0883f876cd4fb8833a8b042cc7b2bb4c4432e85118bd03b35ea7b3702823c8","body":{"detail":"build_record_from_overrides (line 446) is called once per stdin line. When a line omits the 'issuer' key, it calls detect_issuer() at line 483, which spawns 'git config user.email' (line 543) and falls through to 'hg config ui.username' (line 553). For a 1000-line batch with no issuer override, that's ~2000 subprocess forks for an answer that does not change inside one run. Visible as latency on big batches and as noise in process auditing logs.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":478},"end":{"line":485},"content_hash":"16bd138029bc2e9f1ace04ec1f01301b78eecdaec9988e945699aa4a10733afe"},"suggested_fix":"Cache the detected issuer once at the top of run_batch (or in a OnceCell/LazyLock at module scope) and reuse it for every line. Same fix would help run() too if the user pipes record into a tight loop.","summary":"stdin batch re-runs detect_issuer() per line, forking git/hg subprocesses N times for invariant data","tags":["review","perf"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"src/cli/commands/diff.rs","issuer":"mailto:claude-review@anthropic.com","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-05-07T18:24:52.211441Z","id":"6591fa81e85f4ca1e812185bb84d9a5a020d1129adda0d5381b3c76e074afcdb","body":{"detail":"qual_file::discover at line 155 honors .qualignore via the ignore crate (qual_file.rs:191 add_custom_ignore_filename(\".qualignore\")), but load_records_at_ref enumerates blobs straight from the git tree (enumerate_qual_blobs at line 369) with no .qualignore consideration. The doc-comment at line 76-77 claims 'the ref-side enumeration is governed by git itself' to justify --no-ignore not applying there, but git only filters .gitignore'd files at write time — anything tracked at <ref> is in the tree regardless of a current .qualignore. Result: a .qual record present at <ref> in a path that is now .qualignore'd disappears from the new-side scan, so it falls into the Resolved bucket as 'removed (no successor)' even though the file is sitting on disk untouched.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":155},"end":{"line":161},"content_hash":"6ede983c4bbf197d193554361b40b8327c3a5194673d8371c5befa4e3f238f21"},"suggested_fix":"Apply the same ignore::Walk filter to the relative paths returned from enumerate_qual_blobs before loading them, OR document the asymmetry in the --no-ignore help and the diff.md agents page so users aren't surprised by phantom resolutions.","summary":"ref-side enumeration ignores .qualignore, so ignored paths surface asymmetrically as 'Resolved'","tags":["review","bug"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"src/cli/commands/diff.rs","issuer":"mailto:claude-review@anthropic.com","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-05-07T18:24:54.747652Z","id":"33421b18a81c5b13900ec3bce2d68d57546cf9723edf42aeb4c8f50ef91a656f","body":{"detail":"tree.traverse().breadthfirst visits every entry in the tree at <ref>. For a small repo this is fine, but in a monorepo with hundreds of thousands of blobs the cost is O(repo-size) even when there are only a few .qual files. is_qual_path at line 402 is just a path-suffix check, so all the work fetching tree entries just to throw most away is wasted.","kind":"suggestion","span":{"start":{"line":369},"end":{"line":400},"content_hash":"cb831fda1ace50c3aac82f867711e2c6c22ee4777177eddb4cdfc86954be7f91"},"suggested_fix":"gix supports pathspec-filtered traversal; alternatively iterate the tree once and prune subtrees that cannot contain .qual files (no early signal exists, but a depth-first visitor that stops descending into vendored dirs configurable via .qualignore would help). At minimum, benchmark on a >100k-file tree before this becomes a complaint.","summary":"enumerate_qual_blobs walks the entire tree looking at every blob to find .qual files","tags":["review","perf"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"src/cli/commands/diff.rs","issuer":"mailto:claude-review@anthropic.com","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-05-07T18:25:02.835698Z","id":"4cf7a6d3d7986f0b33c10000eb8e45927a60cb7ef97b211a2ff7d59878ffa1ad","body":{"detail":"Line 430-433 builds 'HashMap<&str, &Record>' from filter_map+collect over 'new'. If two HEAD records both supersede the same old id (legal — supersession is acyclic but not unique), the HashMap silently keeps whichever comes last in iteration order, and the Resolved-section closer line shows just that one. The user may not notice the other supersession at all.","kind":"suggestion","span":{"start":{"line":430},"end":{"line":442},"content_hash":"918a0d6117d7bc75d3c50bb5d69cef3dc700607b76ebd6948bd84ea5ffde013d"},"suggested_fix":"Use HashMap<&str, Vec<&Record>> and either join all closers in print_resolved or surface the multiplicity as a warning. At minimum, add a debug_assert!(prior.is_none()) or a stderr 'note:' line so the loss isn't invisible.","summary":"supersedes_index collapses duplicates: only one closer is shown when multiple HEAD records supersede the same id","tags":["review","correctness"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"src/cli/commands/record.rs","issuer":"mailto:claude-review@anthropic.com","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-05-07T18:25:20.512585Z","id":"1f436b7904d5c534cc45b342e96d453cbbe25418ee6d6719339e6abcefad1622","body":{"detail":"When --stdin --continue-on-error --format json sees any failed line, line 296-298 invokes std::process::exit(1) so the top-level main() doesn't add a 'qualifier: ...' line on top of the JSONL summary already on stderr. Effective, but it skips Drop, skips any future shared post-processing in the binary, and embeds the formatting concern (which layer prints the trailing prefix) inside the command implementation. Easy to forget if a future refactor adds a finalizer in lib.rs.","kind":"suggestion","span":{"start":{"line":296},"end":{"line":298},"content_hash":"755a2d5457d7d1510e682c48a00bbbaef3d818716df63d2558edb78990207fc1"},"suggested_fix":"Add a sibling Error variant — e.g. Error::AlreadyReported — that the top-level handler recognizes and exits non-zero without prefixing. Or thread a 'json mode' flag through to the top-level handler so the prefix is suppressed there. Either is more discoverable than process::exit at the leaf.","summary":"stdin batch under --format json calls process::exit(1) to suppress the top-level error prefix — bypasses normal error flow","tags":["review","consistency"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"src/cli/commands/diff.rs","issuer":"mailto:claude-review@anthropic.com","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-05-07T18:25:26.434773Z","id":"ea460cac29b6ea81575b5ca5287bdfd17093c5808a97abaef6a6917f6e2b6c66","body":{"detail":"Defaulting the comparison commit to merge-base(HEAD, ref) is exactly the right call for a PR-style 'what changed?' workflow — listing records that landed on main after the branch forked under 'Resolved' would be misleading. The fallback path (line 145-151) when no merge-base exists prints to stderr and degrades to ref-tip rather than failing, which is friendlier than aborting on a fresh clone with shallow history. The --from-tip flag opts back into the literal compare with documented justification. Worth keeping as-is.","kind":"praise","span":{"start":{"line":132},"end":{"line":153},"content_hash":"7eb354d4c800b570c20fbe5f46d471dff527bdfaafda89931df92a10307018b2"},"summary":"merge-base default with --from-tip escape hatch correctly isolates 'what this branch introduced' from 'what landed on main since fork'","tags":["review","design"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"src/cli/commands/freshness.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@empathic.dev","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-05-08T03:46:18.486799Z","id":"454690068da144f2ca15944df9505654b206ff8fbe1e791d130b03d5f2cc5afb","body":{"kind":"resolve","summary":"review now resolves project root from canonical CWD before discover, and joins the project root with each subject when checking spans","supersedes":"65a84bf71d2265d5e185340d4643c8ec8c3e93f51308cdb5d4c2a3beb706512a"}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"src/cli/commands/record.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-09-25T04:49:32.148012Z","id":"593d6607c17c05ef5b64a35ff44a914d0e3b9e7a5a2918644cfbc885a89138ef","body":{"kind":"resolve","ref":"git:66c1a14","summary":"cli::provenance caches the detected issuer for the process","supersedes":"0c0883f876cd4fb8833a8b042cc7b2bb4c4432e85118bd03b35ea7b3702823c8","tags":["reason:fixed","session:claude-code:6982e520-2dd9-4d9d-b484-c954e628c65c"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"src/cli/commands/record.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-09-25T04:49:36.404782Z","id":"1d14584c9c3c7ef28615040e6bc60c3410ee52cdc35097eb523e3fe791a0d0cc","body":{"kind":"comment","ref":"git:66c1a14","references":"b5b9f7b2f284c6b9337232eb07844ceb19d16531fa7dbaf308ca7901db003143","summary":"issuer and issuer-type plumbing now lives in cli::provenance; the supersession pre-flight is still duplicated","tags":["session:claude-code:6982e520-2dd9-4d9d-b484-c954e628c65c"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"src/cli/commands/record.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-09-25T05:37:01.885166Z","id":"b57883637faf3715e35ea15ce417384ed5570e86626afd6f94e4aba7d3637f50","body":{"kind":"resolve","ref":"git:3f4494e","summary":"issuer plumbing lives in cli::provenance and supersession pre-flight in cli::targets","supersedes":"b5b9f7b2f284c6b9337232eb07844ceb19d16531fa7dbaf308ca7901db003143","tags":["reason:fixed","session:claude-code:6982e520-2dd9-4d9d-b484-c954e628c65c"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"src/cli/commands/record.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-09-25T06:44:22.812945Z","id":"d496d3d58a1604b310d8b665579368399a4500c3b6841da65f78c7d6a4fdbc61","body":{"kind":"resolve","ref":"git:fa59235","summary":"record --stdin now rejects --file; test_record_stdin_rejects_file_and_allow_superseded_flags passes","supersedes":"8e9e728f37b4f84b00c22112318f8154e4fa5a452b770990250d468e63b71f08","tags":["reason:fixed","session:claude-code:6982e520-2dd9-4d9d-b484-c954e628c65c"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"src/cli/commands/compact.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-01T20:16:44.472747Z","id":"2c70122de52aaa031c3ed16cc6fe79289d498e0665548b4e9b0a5c7ce2e8d46c","body":{"detail":"Failure: record a concern on src/a.rs:2, `qualifier reply <id> \"a reply\"`, `qualifier resolve <id> fixed`, then `qualifier compact src/a.rs`. Before compaction `qualifier threads --all` shows one closed thread with the reply under it; afterwards `qualifier threads` lists `[32c842eb] comment src/a.rs a reply` as a new OPEN thread (reproduced against target/debug/qualifier 0.8.0). compact_one calls compact::prune (src/compact.rs:35-53), which keeps only filter_superseded output. A resolve supersedes its target (src/cli/commands/resolve.rs build_resolve), so the root is dropped, while replies point at it via `references`. src/threads.rs:183-192 only treats a record as a reply when its `references` target exists, so every orphaned reply becomes its own origin and an open thread, and the resolve's `supersedes` dangles. The same happens to replies that referenced an earlier version of an edited root. A reply of kind concern or blocker would also be counted by `threads --summary`. compact.md tells users to run this routinely.","kind":"blocker","span":{"start":{"line":69},"end":{"line":108},"content_hash":"dffa13bcec67a26a5033d819953e2ad3e8fd4aededda5aa6ed929dc0b3f26274"},"suggested_fix":"Make prune thread-aware: keep any superseded record that is still the `references` or `supersedes` target of a kept record (iterate to a fixed point), or prune whole closed threads (root, history, replies, resolve) together. Add a regression test in tests/cli_integration.rs that compacts a resolved thread with a reply and asserts `qualifier threads` reports zero open threads.","summary":"compact (prune) deletes resolved and edited thread roots, which detaches their replies and reopens them as new open threads","tags":["review","review:2026-10-01","session:claude-code:906ddd45-2dc1-4a64-9674-56eec33bd6f1"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"src/cli/commands/compact.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-01T20:16:44.472827Z","id":"11ce4c24ddc2fbd4810e113c4e6aeaea2bbe1a95c3f0b70689d7610ea352ab74","body":{"detail":"Failure: in a repo with src/a.rs (two annotations) and an open blocker on src/b.rs, both stored in the directory-level src/.qual, `qualifier compact src/a.rs --snapshot --dry-run` reports `src/.qual: 5 -> 2 record (epoch)`; without --dry-run, src/b.rs's open blocker is replaced by an epoch `Compacted from 1 records` and disappears from `qualifier threads`. run() resolves the artifact only to pick a file (Locator::existing_qual_file), then compact_one snapshots/prunes the whole QualFile, and compact::snapshot (src/compact.rs) emits one epoch per subject in that file. Directory-level .qual is the default layout (AGENTS.md), so this is the common case. src/cli/commands/agents/pages/compact.md:30-37 promises 'Snapshot one artifact's history to a single epoch record' and lines 52-54 say snapshot collapses only 'remaining (non-superseded) records' into 'one epoch record'; the code includes superseded records' IDs and produces one epoch per subject, and the success line hardcodes the singular 'record (epoch)' even when after > 1. Snapshot also folds open blockers and concerns into an epoch with no warning. Records for the artifact that live in a second .qual file (both a 1:1 file and the directory .qual) are not compacted at all.","kind":"blocker","span":{"start":{"line":40},"end":{"line":49},"content_hash":"a749d03c1c5d37c0083e1d0084f48552e06a7b90d3c29d047752c45301d7172b"},"suggested_fix":"When an artifact is given, restrict prune/snapshot to records whose subject is that artifact (across every discovered .qual file containing it) and pass other subjects' records through unchanged. Refuse --snapshot (or require --force) when it would fold open blocker/concern threads. Print the real epoch count, and update compact.md to describe the per-subject and per-file behavior.","summary":"`compact <artifact> --snapshot` collapses every subject in the shared directory .qual, erasing open blockers on other files","tags":["review","review:2026-10-01","session:claude-code:906ddd45-2dc1-4a64-9674-56eec33bd6f1"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"src/cli/commands/show.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-01T20:16:44.472878Z","id":"4a180e508095189d3a35f0720728a70838a5b29fdcd45042cc6be2d878e845e7","body":{"detail":"After `qualifier reply <id> \"a reply\"` and `qualifier resolve <id> fixed`, `qualifier show src/a.rs` prints `Records (1): comment \"a reply\" ...` (reproduced on 0.8.0). The filter here hides superseded records and resolve tombstones, but a resolve supersedes only the root, so the root is hidden while its replies stay 'active'; the threading at lines 107-127 then cannot find the parent and promotes each reply to a root. A reader sees an unexplained comment, or worse, an apparently open reply-kind concern, on a file whose thread was closed. src/cli/commands/agents/pages/show.md:74-76 says 'Resolved records are hidden by default'. show also re-derives threading from `references` alone instead of using crate::threads::build_threads, which already knows open/closed and edit history, so the two commands disagree on what is open.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":56},"end":{"line":66},"content_hash":"73875cea3b6d24f363e4854c5e3cfaa15dc1e2bd067d0c7d17970d178a192f96"},"suggested_fix":"Build the view from threads::build_threads(records) filtered to the subject: by default show open threads (root plus live replies) and drop closed threads entirely; under --all show closed threads marked '(closed)' with their resolve. That also removes the duplicated reply-tree logic.","summary":"show presents replies of a resolved thread as standalone active records with no sign the thread is closed","tags":["review","review:2026-10-01","session:claude-code:906ddd45-2dc1-4a64-9674-56eec33bd6f1"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"src/cli/commands/praise.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-01T20:16:44.472928Z","id":"eabd4b164b88cdcdcc3d57c3547f291cd434b56d12c6ecfde2e84741ce1a29d6","body":{"detail":"src/cli/commands/agents/pages/praise.md:58-63 says praise 'only shows active records — superseded and resolved annotations are filtered out' and that 'the human output strips the mailto: prefix and the domain to show a short name'. In practice, after resolving a thread with one reply, `qualifier praise src/a.rs` prints both `comment \"a reply\"` and `resolve \"fixed\"` with `mailto:t@x.com` (reproduced on 0.8.0). The code applies only filter_superseded (line 50), never drops Kind::Resolve as show does, and prints att.issuer verbatim (line 96); the mailto-stripping lives only in show.rs:156-160. Agents that rely on the page to parse or interpret praise output get a different shape.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":49},"end":{"line":97},"content_hash":"e7583a816c7c0d6885f9ce01ac60d3cca42a0f4030ecd8a0a1cb80c187407319"},"suggested_fix":"Either filter resolve records and closed-thread replies (sharing the thread-aware filter used by show) and reuse show's issuer shortening, or correct praise.md to describe what praise actually prints. Pick one rendering of issuers for both commands.","summary":"praise lists resolve tombstones and orphaned replies, and prints full issuer URIs, contradicting its agents page","tags":["review","review:2026-10-01","session:claude-code:906ddd45-2dc1-4a64-9674-56eec33bd6f1"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"src/cli/commands/freshness.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-01T20:16:44.472970Z","id":"2d2e2c6180161011aef22554c25b001b51e32af9fd082dd2dc9fd47d6672da91","body":{"detail":"From src/, `qualifier review a.rs` prints 'No annotations with content hashes found.' while `qualifier review src/a.rs` reports the annotation (reproduced on 0.8.0). freshness::run compares `record.subject() != subject_filter` directly and builds its own root via find_project_root + qual_file::discover instead of targets::Locator / targets::discover_project, which threads/show/praise/compact use to map CWD-relative arguments onto stored subjects (src/cli/targets.rs:29-100). threads.md documents that 'Locations are relative to the current directory'; review silently returns nothing instead of erroring, so a pre-commit hook run from a subdirectory (the use case review.md:42-45 suggests) reports success while checking nothing. It also accepts no directory or span filter.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":29},"end":{"line":66},"content_hash":"f2026c619e260c77a2999b6fe419761ce58d2a4d7c283967a13285e7982d7368"},"suggested_fix":"Resolve the argument with targets::Locator::from_cwd()?.subject(arg) and discover via targets::discover_project, matching the subject (and descendants for a directory) the same way threads' LocationFilter does. Update review.md's `[subject]` description accordingly.","summary":"`qualifier review <subject>` matches the raw root-relative string, unlike every other command's CWD-relative locations","tags":["review","review:2026-10-01","session:claude-code:906ddd45-2dc1-4a64-9674-56eec33bd6f1"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"src/cli/commands/diff.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-01T20:16:44.473050Z","id":"f9b62efa2db9a963b78c1b1b0a7c6b3f3cb81e881ec6e28af98a3997249d6cb1","body":{"detail":"On a branch, superseding an existing blocker with `qualifier record blocker src/a.rs:3 \"same\" --supersedes <id>` (the remediation review.md:63-65 recommends for drifted spans) makes `qualifier diff main --fail-on blocker` exit 1 with `diff failed: 1 added record(s) match --fail-on blocker`; the old record also appears under Resolved as 'superseded by' (reproduced on 0.8.0). Added is computed per record ID, and an edit gets a new ID. diff.md:86 describes --fail-on as catching added records, and a CI gate that trips on every re-anchor or summary fix of an existing blocker will be disabled by users.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":415},"end":{"line":426},"content_hash":"894d0e8cfd13ea804f54bbf342b5d5068f10ab9191b7dcffd1ec9d6cdf02730d"},"suggested_fix":"Classify a head-side record whose `supersedes` target exists at <ref> (and is not a resolve) as Changed/Edited rather than Added and Resolved, exclude it from --fail-on unless its kind escalated, and document the bucket in diff.md. Reckoning by thread origin (crate::threads) instead of record ID gives this directly.","summary":"diff counts an edit of a pre-existing record as both Added and Resolved, so `--fail-on blocker` fails CI when a blocker is merely re-anchored","tags":["review","review:2026-10-01","session:claude-code:906ddd45-2dc1-4a64-9674-56eec33bd6f1"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"src/cli/commands/diff.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-01T20:16:44.473102Z","id":"906f60a9683271f49db952dd9cd9c191ae0783d643686a88ca615a74350b1fcc","body":{"detail":"enforce_fail_flags compares kind strings exactly; `--fail-on blockers` or `--fail-on Blocker` never matches and the command exits 0 even when blockers were added. apply_filters (lines 184-221) and threads' --kind (src/cli/commands/threads.rs:110-114) behave the same way. Kind::from_str is infallible because custom kinds are allowed (src/annotation.rs:185-202), so nothing ever reports the typo. For a flag whose purpose is gating merges, a silent no-op is the worst failure mode.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":266},"end":{"line":296},"content_hash":"6df6477192acf0b0a8a06c87f832f49bed720c7b03ed55fa2ce7e29cadd804ce"},"suggested_fix":"Warn on stderr for any listed kind that is neither a built-in Kind nor present in any record on either side of the diff (and similarly for threads --kind); optionally add a --strict-kinds mode that turns the warning into an error.","summary":"a misspelled kind in `--fail-on` or `--kind` silently matches nothing, so a CI gate passes","tags":["review","review:2026-10-01","session:claude-code:906ddd45-2dc1-4a64-9674-56eec33bd6f1"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"src/cli/commands/diff.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-01T20:16:44.473143Z","id":"84b71c4c598928b6bb8c7831433a8f787a07860cd5b303ba744af030654d99da","body":{"detail":"In the no-merge-base fallback, effective_oid is the ref tip, but DiffHeader::human (lines 247-261) decides 'no merge-base resolution happened' by comparing base (a full SHA) to input_ref (e.g. 'main'), which is never equal unless the user typed a full SHA, so it prints 'Comparing HEAD against merge-base of main (<sha>)'. The JSON payload carries only `from_tip: false` and `base`, so consumers cannot tell the fallback happened; only a stderr line mentions it.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":136},"end":{"line":153},"content_hash":"d2f6bead9d7b4b1b03f1ff4ec1a870c01b4d28544d0de71f36c1f1057e729c12"},"suggested_fix":"Record the fallback explicitly (e.g. an enum Base { MergeBase, Tip, FallbackTip }) in DiffHeader, use it for the human header, and emit it in JSON (for example `\"comparison\": \"merge-base\" | \"tip\"`).","summary":"when no merge-base exists, the header and JSON still claim a merge-base comparison","tags":["review","review:2026-10-01","session:claude-code:906ddd45-2dc1-4a64-9674-56eec33bd6f1"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"src/cli/commands/diff.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-01T20:16:44.473179Z","id":"3ec580af1e3489c5d00ca7afe51436cafb67e91b3f335835ade41b7754f79921","body":{"detail":"The doc comment says current-tree paths are unioned with ref paths 'so that .qual files deleted on this branch still surface their old records', but the loop `continue`s for any path not in qual_blobs_at_ref, so the result equals iterating qual_blobs_at_ref alone. Deleted files are surfaced because they are in the ref tree, not because of the union. The dead union and misleading comment invite a wrong fix later.","kind":"suggestion","span":{"start":{"line":303},"end":{"line":329},"content_hash":"8ecca149ced5cf56b426a78c34f1118e2cced184c5b92c860d64e49c6c9ee3e3"},"suggested_fix":"Iterate qual_blobs_at_ref directly, drop the new_qual_files parameter, and rewrite the comment to say every .qual blob at <ref> is read.","summary":"load_records_at_ref unions current .qual paths into the set it reads, then skips them all; the comment claims otherwise","tags":["review","review:2026-10-01","session:claude-code:906ddd45-2dc1-4a64-9674-56eec33bd6f1"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"src/cli/commands/show.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-01T20:16:44.473289Z","id":"c9122713daace58fa2abae5b7e7f0f90fe6947fcf0f89fffb90037995715e083","body":{"detail":"The dependency-record branch says dependencies 'remain visible in --format json and via `qualifier graph`'. The graph command and src/graph.rs were removed (SPEC.md §3.4 notes the reference CLI no longer consumes dependency records; src/cli/mod.rs has no Graph variant).","kind":"suggestion","span":{"start":{"line":192},"end":{"line":197},"content_hash":"92885b859d4223ab745c38828ca0be57e292381d5d9e932520022f7de0db3167"},"suggested_fix":"Drop the `qualifier graph` mention: 'They remain visible in --format json.'","summary":"comment points readers to `qualifier graph`, a command that no longer exists","tags":["review","review:2026-10-01","session:claude-code:906ddd45-2dc1-4a64-9674-56eec33bd6f1"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"src/cli/commands/record.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-01T20:17:03.731153Z","id":"6de6e110bc93f9e9284caa96a8da42447e1b2ce6e496eb0df7e657c97da74027","body":{"detail":"Failure: in a git repo, `echo '{\"metabox\":\"1\",\"subject\":\"../escaped.rs\",\"issuer\":\"mailto:t@x\",\"created_at\":\"2026-01-01T00:00:00Z\",\"id\":\"\",\"body\":{\"kind\":\"concern\",\"summary\":\"x\"}}' | qualifier record --stdin` exits 0 and appends the record to `<repo>/../.qual`, a file outside the repository (reproduced at ee6f8d5). A line whose subject is absolute, such as `/tmp/x.rs`, does the same, because `Locator::write_path` (src/cli/targets.rs:112-124) does `root.join(subject)`, and joining an absolute path replaces the root. Overrides lines pass their `location` through `Locator::location`, which rejects paths that leave the root (targets.rs:59-93). Envelope lines take `subject` verbatim from the JSON (record.rs:393-396). They also skip `require_live_id`: an envelope with `\"references\":\"000...0\"` (64 zeros) is accepted under --dry-run, though the --stdin help (record.rs:85-87) says pointers must name a live record. Only `check_supersession` runs, and only when `supersedes` is set.","kind":"blocker","span":{"start":{"line":393},"end":{"line":416},"content_hash":"13205d3f9a3618e59e21d66ed089b819f0806b42e12e4254f5afd59903412bd8"},"suggested_fix":"In plan_one, after deserializing an envelope, run its subject through `locator.subject()` (or reject subjects that are absolute or contain `..`). Run `require_live_id` on its `supersedes`/`references` the same way `build_record_from_overrides` does. As a backstop, have `Locator::write_path` refuse any path that is not under `root`.","summary":"record --stdin envelope lines skip location normalization and pointer checks, so they can write outside the project root and carry dangling references","tags":["review","review:2026-10-01","session:claude-code:906ddd45-2dc1-4a64-9674-56eec33bd6f1"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"src/cli/commands/emit.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-01T20:17:03.731267Z","id":"22574e8a207ae384b649d4ecf701859bb67694c47aa0c5fc00b6d1a11bf8ff2a","body":{"detail":"Running `qualifier emit annotation src/a.rs --body '{\"kind\":\"comment\",\"summary\":\"e\"}'` from inside `src/` stores subject `src/a.rs` and creates `src/src/.qual` (reproduced at ee6f8d5). `record` run from the same directory with the same argument stores `src/src/a.rs` in `src/src/.qual` via `targets::Locator`. Either way, the two write commands disagree about what a subject argument means. emit calls the older `qual_file::resolve_qual_path` (emit.rs:81-82 and 194), which checks `{subject}.qual` and `{parent}/.qual` relative to CWD and does not normalize `..` or absolute paths, so emit can also write outside the project root.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":81},"end":{"line":84},"content_hash":"076187b933c9837e24f73533ca9204a6ec8bc47779fe3ed7a71e15832aaf6087"},"suggested_fix":"Use `targets::Locator::from_cwd()` in emit: normalize the subject with `locator.subject()` and pick the file with `locator.write_path()`, as record does. Then decide whether `qual_file::resolve_qual_path` is still needed by any caller.","summary":"emit resolves subjects and .qual paths relative to the current directory, unlike record/reply/resolve, which are root-relative","tags":["review","review:2026-10-01","session:claude-code:906ddd45-2dc1-4a64-9674-56eec33bd6f1"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"src/cli/commands/emit.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-01T20:17:03.731299Z","id":"4d31ce37c60cc82997795e166c0f588b72682fa4aa80d01d9e0f02c5cdf15935","body":{"detail":"`printf '<valid annotation envelope>\\nnot json\\n' | qualifier emit --stdin` appends the first record, then exits 1 with `stdin line 2: expected ident ...` (reproduced at ee6f8d5: the first record is in the .qual). `record --stdin` documents the opposite contract (record.rs:95-101): every line is validated first, nothing is written if any fails, and --dry-run and --continue-on-error are available. A caller that retries a failed `emit --stdin` batch duplicates the lines that went through. emit also runs no supersession, cycle, or liveness checks on batch lines.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":156},"end":{"line":211},"content_hash":"59d1598a2edaf8754efce36291faaca2f3495501de073513aa51a659f5e7e834"},"suggested_fix":"Give emit --stdin the same plan-then-write structure as record's run_batch (or share it): parse and validate every line, report all errors, and append only when the batch is clean. Alternatively, document in emit's --stdin help that the batch is not atomic.","summary":"emit --stdin writes line by line and stops at the first bad line, leaving a partial batch on disk","tags":["review","review:2026-10-01","session:claude-code:906ddd45-2dc1-4a64-9674-56eec33bd6f1"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"src/cli/commands/record.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-01T20:17:03.731381Z","id":"38acb48f552f102a3785403d10ac2d4b163036dbe6924c4de02f9866663a343a","body":{"detail":"`echo '{\"kind\":\"concern\",\"location\":\"src/a.rs\",\"message\":\"m\",\"suggestedfix\":\"lost\",\"supersede\":\"x\"}' | qualifier record --stdin --dry-run --format json` succeeds and plans a plain concern with no suggested_fix and no supersedes (reproduced at ee6f8d5). A misspelled `supersedes` or `references` is the costly case: the intended re-anchor or reply becomes an unlinked new record, the old one stays open, and nothing is reported. The same happens when `tags` holds a non-string element (tags_field drops it, record.rs:423-432) or `span` is not a string (record.rs:537). The batch format is aimed at agents (see `qualifier agents batch`), and agents are the callers most likely to guess key names.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":517},"end":{"line":560},"content_hash":"f3392a13c9a3c94a20dc34819aa9c4c7345ec0267ed0800f03014ca0c4f67171"},"suggested_fix":"Reject an overrides object that has any key outside the documented set (kind, location, message, detail, suggested_fix, tags, issuer, issuer_type, ref, supersedes, references, span), and name the key in the error. Also reject wrongly typed values instead of dropping them. Deserializing into a `#[serde(deny_unknown_fields)]` struct would do both.","summary":"record --stdin overrides lines silently ignore unknown keys, so a typo in a pointer or field drops it without error","tags":["review","review:2026-10-01","session:claude-code:906ddd45-2dc1-4a64-9674-56eec33bd6f1"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"src/cli/commands/record.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-01T20:17:03.731473Z","id":"8e37ccfefd536671961a43faa4eb710c18f62cf230f92c5d2aa6c62cc4a18015","body":{"detail":"record, reply, and resolve declare `format: String` with default \"human\" and compare it to \"json\". `qualifier record comment src/a.rs x --format jsn` exits 0 and prints human output (reproduced at ee6f8d5). A script that parses stdout as JSON then fails later with a confusing parse error, while the record has already been written. In --stdin mode, the format also decides whether errors go to stderr as JSONL (record.rs:450-466).","kind":"suggestion","span":{"start":{"line":116},"end":{"line":119},"content_hash":"e3f10e93720966dd2dc205d35b414b9607e33cd9e9fda318e134c357a44a9517"},"suggested_fix":"Make the format a `clap::ValueEnum` (`Human`, `Json`) shared by every command, so clap rejects unknown values before anything is written.","summary":"--format accepts any string and silently falls back to human output on a typo","tags":["review","review:2026-10-01","session:claude-code:906ddd45-2dc1-4a64-9674-56eec33bd6f1"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"src/cli/commands/record.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-01T20:17:03.731540Z","id":"3d3f0984d8669f38f49fd30df8aa363e84e5bc8d26ad4185eea48631c4b7cb06","body":{"detail":"run() (record.rs:145-212) and build_record_from_overrides (record.rs:517-584) each parse kind and location, apply the span override, compute content_hash, resolve issuer and issuer_type, check reason tags, check supersedes and references, and build the same `Annotation` literal. The span-display formatting is also duplicated (record.rs:219-228 and 488-497). The copies have already drifted: the single path validates supersession against one file (`preflight_supersession`), while the batch path validates against every record in the project (`check_supersession(view.all_records(), ...)`). The next field added to AnnotationBody has to be wired in four places in this file, plus reply.rs and resolve.rs.","kind":"suggestion","span":{"start":{"line":145},"end":{"line":237},"content_hash":"c2f800abbf53150b30996563657c251c6328bb5a9d4c4d0675b2b458e0929a0a"},"suggested_fix":"Collect the inputs into one struct (like reply's ReplyInput) and build the annotation in a single function, `build_annotation(input, files, locator)`. Have run() call it with the on-disk files and run_batch call it with the BatchView, so both paths share pointer and supersession checks. Move span display into a `Span` Display helper.","summary":"Single-record and batch annotation construction are two near-identical copies, and they already disagree on pointer checks","tags":["review","review:2026-10-01","session:claude-code:906ddd45-2dc1-4a64-9674-56eec33bd6f1"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"src/cli/commands/compact.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-01T20:18:47.299492Z","id":"7722322486363bcf6a7b85442d31d9c30d5924fb7cb3051940cb6f7da2e195b6","body":{"detail":"Reproduced with target/debug/qualifier 0.8.0 in a scratch git repo: record concern src/a.rs:2, reply, resolve, record blocker src/b.rs:1. Before: `threads` = 1 open (b.rs); `threads --all` shows the a.rs thread (closed) with the reply under it. After `qualifier compact src/a.rs`: '4 -> 3 records (1 superseded, pruned)' and `threads` lists `[77e49af4] comment src/a.rs a reply` as open, '2 threads (2 open)'. Cause: src/compact.rs:35-53 prune keeps only filter_superseded output; src/threads.rs:183-185 is_reply requires the references target to exist.","kind":"comment","references":"2c70122de52aaa031c3ed16cc6fe79289d498e0665548b4e9b0a5c7ce2e8d46c","summary":"confirmed: compact prune drops a resolved root and its reply becomes a new open thread","tags":["verified:confirmed","session:claude-code:906ddd45-2dc1-4a64-9674-56eec33bd6f1"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"src/cli/commands/compact.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-01T20:18:47.299527Z","id":"e0a67d1fb13540db38e60ad037c857d4958af0d7fcae72d05bc611e933feecaf","body":{"detail":"Same scratch repo (a.rs thread + open blocker on src/b.rs, both in src/.qual): `qualifier compact src/a.rs --snapshot --dry-run` prints 'src/.qual: 4 -> 2 record (epoch)'; without --dry-run src/.qual holds two epochs (subjects src/a.rs and src/b.rs) and `qualifier threads` reports '0 threads (0 open)' -- the b.rs blocker is gone. src/cli/commands/compact.rs:40-50 uses the artifact only to pick the file, then compact_one (line 69+) snapshots the whole QualFile; line 86 hardcodes 'record (epoch)' though after=2.","kind":"comment","references":"11ce4c24ddc2fbd4810e113c4e6aeaea2bbe1a95c3f0b70689d7610ea352ab74","summary":"confirmed: compact <artifact> --snapshot rewrites every subject in the directory .qual and erases another file's open blocker","tags":["verified:confirmed","session:claude-code:906ddd45-2dc1-4a64-9674-56eec33bd6f1"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"src/cli/commands/show.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-01T20:18:47.299541Z","id":"73b7c435d7729b705767212638bdee5473d536395cf17d7d0ec0f857b12e991b","body":{"detail":"Scratch repo after reply + resolve on a src/a.rs concern: `qualifier show src/a.rs` prints 'Records (1): comment \"a reply\" t (ai) 2026-10-01 77e49af4' with no closed marker, while `threads --all` marks the same thread '(closed)'. src/cli/commands/agents/pages/show.md:74-76 says resolved records are hidden by default.","kind":"comment","references":"4a180e508095189d3a35f0720728a70838a5b29fdcd45042cc6be2d878e845e7","summary":"confirmed: show lists a closed thread's reply as an active standalone record","tags":["verified:confirmed","session:claude-code:906ddd45-2dc1-4a64-9674-56eec33bd6f1"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"src/cli/commands/praise.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-01T20:18:47.299553Z","id":"44d28b03329f86641ff0275eeee2dfb23b9a8b56b71461751164cfe30a440331","body":{"detail":"Scratch repo: `qualifier praise src/a.rs` prints 'comment \"a reply\" mailto:t@x.com ...' and 'resolve \"fixed\" mailto:t@x.com ...'. praise.rs:50 applies only filter_superseded and line 96 prints att.issuer verbatim; the strip_prefix(\"mailto:\") shortening exists only in show.rs:156-160. praise.md:58-63 claims resolved annotations are filtered and mailto/domain are stripped.","kind":"comment","references":"eabd4b164b88cdcdcc3d57c3547f291cd434b56d12c6ecfde2e84741ce1a29d6","summary":"confirmed: praise prints resolve records and closed-thread replies with full mailto: issuers, contrary to praise.md","tags":["verified:confirmed","session:claude-code:906ddd45-2dc1-4a64-9674-56eec33bd6f1"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"src/cli/commands/freshness.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-01T20:18:47.299563Z","id":"792e5e3c67ac6cd6c523eae4602dc244a20bca00e7ff795b7bb55cdcd67f5405","body":{"detail":"From src/ in a scratch repo: `qualifier review a.rs` -> 'No annotations with content hashes found.'; `qualifier review src/a.rs` -> FRESH src/a.rs:3; `qualifier show a.rs` from src/ shows src/a.rs. freshness.rs:29-33 uses find_project_root + qual_file::discover and line 61-64 compares record.subject() to the raw argument. Note: the review.md:42-45 example feeds `git diff --name-only`, which emits root-relative paths, so that specific flow works; the inconsistency with other commands is what holds, and concern is the right kind.","kind":"comment","references":"2d2e2c6180161011aef22554c25b001b51e32af9fd082dd2dc9fd47d6672da91","summary":"confirmed: review matches the subject string root-relative while show/threads resolve it CWD-relative","tags":["verified:confirmed","session:claude-code:906ddd45-2dc1-4a64-9674-56eec33bd6f1"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"src/cli/commands/diff.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-01T20:18:47.299577Z","id":"417820bc9a81e2e0c43aafe87b15e6db4494f292c7314254e2a922beb2477ad2","body":{"detail":"Scratch repo: blocker on src/a.rs:3 committed on main; on branch feat, `qualifier record blocker src/a.rs:3 \"same\" --supersedes <id>`; `qualifier diff main --fail-on blocker` lists it under 'Added on this branch (1)' and the old one under 'Resolved ... superseded by fc9b3a09', then exits 1 with 'diff failed: 1 added record(s) match --fail-on blocker'. enforce_fail_flags (diff.rs:266-296) checks diff.added by kind only.","kind":"comment","references":"f9b62efa2db9a963b78c1b1b0a7c6b3f3cb81e881ec6e28af98a3997249d6cb1","summary":"confirmed: re-anchoring an existing blocker via --supersedes trips diff --fail-on blocker","tags":["verified:confirmed","session:claude-code:906ddd45-2dc1-4a64-9674-56eec33bd6f1"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"src/cli/commands/diff.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-01T20:18:47.299590Z","id":"ba3ccb6ccad0cf3b51b941910076ae1c2770867506ad566c084ccfa93c8ebb02","body":{"detail":"Scratch repo with an added blocker: `qualifier diff main --fail-on blockers` exits 0, `--fail-on blocker` exits 1, no warning either way. diff.rs:280-288 compares kind strings exactly; apply_filters (diff.rs:184-221) does the same; threads.rs:110-114 parses via Kind::from_str which is Infallible (src/annotation.rs:185-202) mapping unknowns to Kind::Custom.","kind":"comment","references":"906f60a9683271f49db952dd9cd9c191ae0783d643686a88ca615a74350b1fcc","summary":"confirmed: --fail-on with a misspelled kind silently passes","tags":["verified:confirmed","session:claude-code:906ddd45-2dc1-4a64-9674-56eec33bd6f1"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"src/cli/commands/diff.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-01T20:18:47.299601Z","id":"6a03907114b84339f87c4d1e03e22a168d5838b685319db4a9554b4659d8db3b","body":{"detail":"Scratch repo, orphan branch `lone` vs main: stderr prints 'no merge-base between HEAD and 'main', comparing to ref tip' but stdout header is 'Comparing HEAD against merge-base of main (e9c6c26)'. diff.rs:166-170 sets base = effective_oid.to_string() (full SHA), so the `self.base == self.input_ref` fallback branch at diff.rs:251 never fires for a named ref; JSON (diff.rs:737-738) carries only base and from_tip.","kind":"comment","references":"84b71c4c598928b6bb8c7831433a8f787a07860cd5b303ba744af030654d99da","summary":"confirmed: with no merge-base the header still says 'merge-base of main'","tags":["verified:confirmed","session:claude-code:906ddd45-2dc1-4a64-9674-56eec33bd6f1"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"src/cli/commands/diff.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-01T20:18:47.299611Z","id":"931e9775affc0fc853e895313a627fc49c465dcb936f27638ec459c5637c5a19","body":{"detail":"diff.rs:313-326 inserts new_qual_files paths into `paths`, then the loop does `let Some(blob_oid) = qual_blobs_at_ref.get(&rel) else { continue; }`, so only keys of qual_blobs_at_ref ever contribute; the comment at diff.rs:303-305 attributes deleted-file coverage to the union.","kind":"comment","references":"3ec580af1e3489c5d00ca7afe51436cafb67e91b3f335835ade41b7754f79921","summary":"confirmed: the union with current-tree paths is dead code and the doc comment is misleading","tags":["verified:confirmed","session:claude-code:906ddd45-2dc1-4a64-9674-56eec33bd6f1"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"src/cli/commands/show.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-01T20:18:47.299644Z","id":"e8a10e14b02c98c932f1cab7c1f4b12e4b85637b0b2b077f6e861bc3aeab23de","body":{"detail":"show.rs:196 reads 'They remain visible in `--format json` and via `qualifier graph`.'; `grep -n Graph src/cli/mod.rs` returns nothing and src/graph.rs does not exist.","kind":"comment","references":"c9122713daace58fa2abae5b7e7f0f90fe6947fcf0f89fffb90037995715e083","summary":"confirmed: comment references the removed `qualifier graph` command","tags":["verified:confirmed","session:claude-code:906ddd45-2dc1-4a64-9674-56eec33bd6f1"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"src/cli/commands/record.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-01T20:19:08.965389Z","id":"2b151e7d303fc9c387765150a56faacfe0177f230345722ba75d93b20232dac6","body":{"detail":"In a scratch git repo built at ee6f8d5 (target/debug/qualifier): piping the envelope with subject '../escaped.rs' to `qualifier record --stdin` printed `recorded concern ../escaped.rs` with rc=0 and the record landed in <repo>/../.qual, outside the repo. An envelope on src/a.rs with references of 64 zeros passed `--dry-run` (rc=0). Code: plan_one (src/cli/commands/record.rs:393-396) deserializes the envelope and calls only finalize_record; require_live_id is reached only via build_record_from_overrides (record.rs:567-572); Locator::write_path (src/cli/targets.rs:112-124) does root.join(subject) with no containment check.","kind":"comment","references":"6de6e110bc93f9e9284caa96a8da42447e1b2ce6e496eb0df7e657c97da74027","summary":"confirmed: envelope lines on record --stdin bypass Locator::subject and require_live_id","tags":["verified:confirmed","session:claude-code:906ddd45-2dc1-4a64-9674-56eec33bd6f1"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"src/cli/commands/emit.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-01T20:19:08.965437Z","id":"e4ade0742eb3cea46ec7be562a0a28e36a57b8dd4f49f277229835a94c29d6d1","body":{"detail":"In a scratch git repo built at ee6f8d5 (target/debug/qualifier), from src/: `emit annotation src/a.rs --body ...` stored subject `src/a.rs` and created src/src/.qual; `record comment src/a.rs r` from the same dir printed `comment src/src/a.rs r`. emit.rs:81-82 calls qual_file::resolve_qual_path(record.subject(), ...) with no Locator normalization; record.rs uses targets::Locator::location/write_path.","kind":"comment","references":"22574e8a207ae384b649d4ecf701859bb67694c47aa0c5fc00b6d1a11bf8ff2a","summary":"confirmed: emit resolves subject and .qual path relative to CWD, record resolves relative to the root","tags":["verified:confirmed","session:claude-code:906ddd45-2dc1-4a64-9674-56eec33bd6f1"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"src/cli/commands/emit.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-01T20:19:08.965451Z","id":"5a2f05fa4a3e8cf2550da41c3627b0b21d82637ddaf625633874a4821ff06ff3","body":{"detail":"In a scratch git repo built at ee6f8d5 (target/debug/qualifier): printf '<valid envelope>\\nnot json\\n' | qualifier emit --stdin printed `emitted annotation lib/b.rs id: bf71e0b9`, then `stdin line 2: expected ident at line 1 column 2`, rc=1; lib/.qual went from 1 to 2 lines. emit.rs run_batch (156-211) calls qual_file::append inside the per-line loop (line ~198) and returns on the first error; no supersession or liveness checks are run.","kind":"comment","references":"4d31ce37c60cc82997795e166c0f588b72682fa4aa80d01d9e0f02c5cdf15935","summary":"confirmed: emit --stdin appends line by line and leaves a partial batch on failure","tags":["verified:confirmed","session:claude-code:906ddd45-2dc1-4a64-9674-56eec33bd6f1"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"src/cli/commands/record.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-01T20:19:08.965474Z","id":"b450a24c55506c6443f7b8cc4076d1af293cfb52ed0772197b73879ca8fe72c9","body":{"detail":"In a scratch git repo built at ee6f8d5 (target/debug/qualifier): the line with `suggestedfix` and `supersede` keys under `record --stdin --dry-run --format json` produced a plain concern with no suggested_fix or supersedes, rc=0. build_record_from_overrides (record.rs:517-584) reads fields by name via str_field/tags_field (record.rs:419-432), which return None or filter_map on non-strings, and never inspects other keys; span uses .as_str() (record.rs:537).","kind":"comment","references":"38acb48f552f102a3785403d10ac2d4b163036dbe6924c4de02f9866663a343a","summary":"confirmed: unknown or mistyped keys on overrides lines are silently dropped","tags":["verified:confirmed","session:claude-code:906ddd45-2dc1-4a64-9674-56eec33bd6f1"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"src/cli/commands/record.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-01T20:19:08.965506Z","id":"1009a76ca5e3e4cd861fc662963d05ebd610466358810fa8e2236b5b5f721515","body":{"detail":"record.rs:116-119 declares `format: String` with default_value \"human\"; output branches on `== \"json\"` (record.rs:216, 450-466). In a scratch git repo built at ee6f8d5 (target/debug/qualifier): `record comment src/a.rs x --format jsn` wrote the record and printed human output, rc=0.","kind":"comment","references":"8e37ccfefd536671961a43faa4eb710c18f62cf230f92c5d2aa6c62cc4a18015","summary":"confirmed: --format is a free String and a typo silently yields human output","tags":["verified:confirmed","session:claude-code:906ddd45-2dc1-4a64-9674-56eec33bd6f1"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"src/cli/commands/record.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-01T20:19:08.965531Z","id":"6fc6a2dc57d179a5649bc48dc70737eb2c2b588af8a67eef879633af4cdca680","body":{"detail":"run() (src/cli/commands/record.rs:122-237) and build_record_from_overrides (record.rs:517-584) repeat kind/location parsing, span override, content hash, issuer/issuer_type, reason tags, pointer checks and the Annotation literal; span display is duplicated at record.rs:219-228 and 488-497. run() checks supersession with targets::preflight_supersession on one file (record.rs:213-215) while plan_one uses check_supersession(view.all_records(), ...) (record.rs:413-415); the cross-subject reproduction in finding ee3f6adb shows the divergence in behavior.","kind":"comment","references":"3d3f0984d8669f38f49fd30df8aa363e84e5bc8d26ad4185eea48631c4b7cb06","summary":"confirmed: single and batch annotation construction are duplicated and their supersession checks differ","tags":["verified:confirmed","session:claude-code:906ddd45-2dc1-4a64-9674-56eec33bd6f1"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"src/cli/commands/compact.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-01T20:33:23.212315Z","id":"97590e3e08041065c60877a303823e14bb78ecddd6756a0244da01ff700fb1d0","body":{"detail":"Evidence: src/cli/commands/compact.rs:86 hardcodes 'record (epoch)'; confirming reply e0a67d1f: `compact src/a.rs --snapshot` left `threads` at '0 threads (0 open)' after erasing the src/b.rs blocker","kind":"comment","references":"11ce4c24ddc2fbd4810e113c4e6aeaea2bbe1a95c3f0b70689d7610ea352ab74","summary":"Resolution: Confirmed. In src/cli/commands/compact.rs run() (lines 40-50), when an artifact is given, split the QualFile records by subject. compact_one (69+) should prune or snapshot only that subject's records, across every .qual file that holds the subject, and write the other subjects' records back unchanged. Refuse --snapshot without --force when a folded thread is an open blocker or concern. Print the real epoch count instead of the hardcoded 'record (epoch)' at line 86. Update agents/pages/compact.md:30-37,52-54 to describe the per-subject behavior.","tags":["session:claude-code:906ddd45-2dc1-4a64-9674-56eec33bd6f1"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"src/cli/commands/emit.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-01T20:33:23.212325Z","id":"4f36ff416b6abf9b60fd9c67f896217451d8a78eb5a08f0a1a9128f1a8a20eb2","body":{"detail":"Evidence: src/cli/commands/emit.rs:81-82 calls qual_file::resolve_qual_path(record.subject(), ...) without Locator; confirming reply: emit from src/ created src/src/.qual with subject src/a.rs","kind":"comment","references":"22574e8a207ae384b649d4ecf701859bb67694c47aa0c5fc00b6d1a11bf8ff2a","summary":"Resolution: Confirmed. In src/cli/commands/emit.rs:81-82 and 194, replace qual_file::resolve_qual_path with targets::Locator::from_cwd(). Normalize with locator.subject(), which also rejects paths outside the root, and pick the file with locator.write_path(), as record.rs does. Then remove resolve_qual_path if nothing else calls it.","tags":["session:claude-code:906ddd45-2dc1-4a64-9674-56eec33bd6f1"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"src/cli/commands/emit.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-01T20:33:23.212335Z","id":"5dc8c72ba4bf11749e468fa0b9bb00d1a6fb08834811adf460981c770c8e5bd5","body":{"detail":"Evidence: `qualifier emit https://example.com/custom/v1 test.rs --body '{\"foo\":\"bar\"}'` in a scratch repo wrote a line with \"id\":\"\" (target/debug/qualifier at HEAD 1ec94ac)","kind":"comment","references":"d7b8f76adcbb216d79c38355947ea09f8936f77b8c71601d5b88382b98454962","summary":"Resolution: Confirmed. Give Record::Unknown a real ID. Add an Unknown arm to annotation::finalize_record (src/annotation.rs:831, currently `other => other`). It should set id to \"\", serialize the envelope in MCF order (see 12542525), BLAKE3-hash it, and store the hash in `id`. Add a golden-ID test for a custom-type record in tests/integration.rs.","tags":["session:claude-code:906ddd45-2dc1-4a64-9674-56eec33bd6f1"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"src/cli/commands/emit.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-01T20:33:23.212345Z","id":"1d6ae6b87bb6871ed9c30fcc2073d26669e7e9a8710e4474f0457ad84785121e","body":{"detail":"Evidence: the same emit command wrote {\"body\":...,\"created_at\":...,\"id\":\"\",\"issuer\":...,\"metabox\":\"1\",...} with keys in alphabetical order, contrary to SPEC.md:411-413 (envelope field order)","kind":"comment","references":"12542525d52f83be6475df6bc3d6fb7e2aca3740a603987f1ba741fe2e4003c5","summary":"Resolution: Confirmed. Do not turn on serde_json's preserve_order: it would also keep insertion order inside body, which breaks MCF rule 3 (body keys sorted alphabetically). Instead, serialize Unknown records through a typed view struct whose fields follow the envelope order (metabox, type, subject, issuer, issuer_type opt, created_at, id, body: Value), like AnnotationCanonicalView. Use it both when writing Unknown records and when hashing them for d7b8f76a. Then build_record in src/cli/commands/emit.rs:127-151 can drop the Map.","tags":["session:claude-code:906ddd45-2dc1-4a64-9674-56eec33bd6f1"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"src/cli/commands/emit.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-01T20:33:23.212356Z","id":"e43f8317fc431a1472fbed0b515069637cce53d77c36bf3ff5f95164b435e6f6","body":{"detail":"Evidence: src/cli/commands/emit.rs run_batch calls qual_file::append inside the per-line loop and returns on the first error; confirming reply: lib/.qual grew from 1 to 2 lines while the command exited 1","kind":"comment","references":"4d31ce37c60cc82997795e166c0f588b72682fa4aa80d01d9e0f02c5cdf15935","summary":"Resolution: Confirmed. Restructure emit's run_batch (src/cli/commands/emit.rs:156-211) to plan first and write second, like record's run_batch. Parse, finalize and validate every line, including the supersession and liveness checks against the project. Report every error, and call qual_file::append only when the whole batch is clean.","tags":["session:claude-code:906ddd45-2dc1-4a64-9674-56eec33bd6f1"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"src/cli/commands/record.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-01T20:33:23.212365Z","id":"a1ad42543113921b4f2872c54710f46cb8830611d0c40b6c958e31e91af616cc","body":{"detail":"Evidence: src/cli/commands/record.rs:116-119 declares `format: String` with default_value \"human\"; confirming reply: `--format jsn` exited 0 and printed human output","kind":"comment","references":"8e37ccfefd536671961a43faa4eb710c18f62cf230f92c5d2aa6c62cc4a18015","summary":"Resolution: Confirmed. Add a shared `#[derive(clap::ValueEnum)] enum Format { Human, Json }`, for example in src/cli/output.rs. Replace `format: String` in record.rs:116-119, reply.rs, resolve.rs and every other command that has a --format flag, and change the `== \"json\"` comparisons to matches on the enum.","tags":["session:claude-code:906ddd45-2dc1-4a64-9674-56eec33bd6f1"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"src/cli/commands/record.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-01T20:33:23.212374Z","id":"b88867084aa3f9e3e97475c9c539f27040d61280255628cf97000840b7456454","body":{"detail":"Evidence: src/cli/commands/record.rs:213-215 (preflight_supersession on one file) vs record.rs:413-415 (check_supersession over view.all_records())","kind":"comment","references":"3d3f0984d8669f38f49fd30df8aa363e84e5bc8d26ad4185eea48631c4b7cb06","summary":"Resolution: Confirmed. Move the inputs into one struct (like ReplyInput) and add a single `build_annotation(input, records_view, locator)` in src/cli/commands/record.rs. Call it from run() (145-212) and from build_record_from_overrides (517-584) so both paths share pointer and supersession checks against the project. Move span display (219-228, 488-497) into a Display impl on Span. Do this together with ee3f6adb.","tags":["session:claude-code:906ddd45-2dc1-4a64-9674-56eec33bd6f1"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"src/cli/commands/record.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-01T20:33:23.212382Z","id":"e749a940b660bdd8dec2ae8f464714ff8196e75ef988bfd993cbbfde9161bb69","body":{"detail":"Evidence: `grep -n process::exit src/cli/commands/record.rs` prints 365: std::process::exit(1); (inside `if format == \"json\"`)","kind":"comment","references":"1f436b7904d5c534cc45b342e96d453cbbe25418ee6d6719339e6abcefad1622","summary":"Resolution: Still present, now at src/cli/commands/record.rs:364-366, not 296-298. Add `Error::AlreadyReported` to the crate Error enum and return it there instead of calling process::exit(1). In src/cli/mod.rs (around line 125/150), exit 1 for that variant without printing the 'qualifier:' prefix. The agents `exit(2)` path could use the same variant.","tags":["session:claude-code:906ddd45-2dc1-4a64-9674-56eec33bd6f1"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"src/cli/commands/record.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-01T20:33:23.212390Z","id":"7081b7e69c9bc59e7e2216fecd1600f697700f4f075dd636f7ec1e473f35192c","body":{"detail":"Evidence: src/cli/targets.rs:112-124 does root.join(subject) with no containment check; confirming reply: an envelope with subject '../escaped.rs' was written to <repo>/../.qual with rc=0","kind":"comment","references":"6de6e110bc93f9e9284caa96a8da42447e1b2ce6e496eb0df7e657c97da74027","summary":"Resolution: Confirmed. In plan_one (src/cli/commands/record.rs:393-396), after deserializing an envelope, pass its subject through locator.subject() and run require_live_id on its supersedes and references, as build_record_from_overrides does at 567-572. As a backstop, make Locator::write_path (src/cli/targets.rs:112-124) return an error for any path not under root.","tags":["session:claude-code:906ddd45-2dc1-4a64-9674-56eec33bd6f1"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"src/cli/commands/record.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-01T20:33:23.212400Z","id":"af779b164e7865df6d16d1c479a585d8d963c0f3fcdf8f435d5db905f2ad1276","body":{"detail":"Evidence: src/cli/commands/record.rs:419-432 tags_field filter_maps non-strings and no code checks for unknown keys; confirming reply: the `suggestedfix`/`supersede` line was planned as a plain concern with rc=0","kind":"comment","references":"38acb48f552f102a3785403d10ac2d4b163036dbe6924c4de02f9866663a343a","summary":"Resolution: Confirmed. Deserialize overrides lines into a `#[serde(deny_unknown_fields)]` struct (kind, location, message, detail, suggested_fix, tags: Vec<String>, issuer, issuer_type, ref, supersedes, references, span: String). Use it in place of str_field/tags_field (src/cli/commands/record.rs:419-432, 537), so unknown keys and wrong value types fail with the key named in the error.","tags":["session:claude-code:906ddd45-2dc1-4a64-9674-56eec33bd6f1"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"src/cli/commands/diff.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-01T20:33:23.212475Z","id":"ce26e0b397038d135100f67be5c571006e77f78efc81e445cb581767684f1b11","body":{"detail":"Evidence: src/cli/commands/diff.rs:136-153 (merge_base with eprintln fallback to ref_oid; from_tip short-circuit)","kind":"comment","references":"ea460cac29b6ea81575b5ca5287bdfd17093c5808a97abaef6a6917f6e2b6c66","summary":"Resolution: No change requested and the praise still holds: merge-base by default, --from-tip to opt out, and falling back to the tip instead of failing. Leave the code as is. Note that its 'degrades to ref-tip' point has a mislabelled header in that fallback; the fix is tracked in 84b71c4c.","tags":["session:claude-code:906ddd45-2dc1-4a64-9674-56eec33bd6f1"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"src/cli/commands/diff.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-01T20:33:23.212484Z","id":"632c85261cdf0af5e214d6a8cfb36718a9ddd716748925fd678290b793c5aab2","body":{"detail":"Evidence: src/cli/commands/diff.rs:166-170 sets base = effective_oid.to_string() (a full SHA), so the fallback branch at diff.rs:251 never fires for a named ref","kind":"comment","references":"84b71c4c598928b6bb8c7831433a8f787a07860cd5b303ba744af030654d99da","summary":"Resolution: In diff.rs:136-153, return which comparison was used along with effective_oid (an enum Comparison { MergeBase, Tip, FallbackTip }). Store it in DiffHeader instead of the `base == input_ref` test at diff.rs:251. Use it in DiffHeader::human, printing 'Comparing HEAD against main (tip; no merge-base)' for the fallback, and emit `\"comparison\": \"merge-base\"|\"tip\"|\"fallback-tip\"` in print_json.","tags":["session:claude-code:906ddd45-2dc1-4a64-9674-56eec33bd6f1"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"src/cli/commands/diff.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-01T20:33:23.212493Z","id":"25fd54627e29462d60819638a8ce54d800517430f2091e6d77ae6dcb1807b974","body":{"detail":"Evidence: src/qual_file.rs:193 (`add_custom_ignore_filename(\".qualignore\")` in discover) vs src/cli/commands/diff.rs:369-400 (enumerate_qual_blobs applies no ignore filter)","kind":"comment","references":"6591fa81e85f4ca1e812185bb84d9a5a020d1129adda0d5381b3c76e074afcdb","summary":"Resolution: Unless --no-ignore is set, filter the paths returned by enumerate_qual_blobs (diff.rs:313) through the same .gitignore/.qualignore matcher that qual_file::discover uses, so both sides of the diff see the same set of files. Then rewrite the --no-ignore doc at diff.rs:75-77 to say it applies to both sides.","tags":["session:claude-code:906ddd45-2dc1-4a64-9674-56eec33bd6f1"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"src/cli/commands/diff.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-01T20:33:23.212501Z","id":"b9bba0d9d8a00f55eba26e66035d1fb02accd22e1bed469f50746e2e3b4047e6","body":{"detail":"Evidence: src/cli/commands/diff.rs:280-288 (exact string compare of kinds, no validation); Kind::from_str is infallible at src/annotation.rs:185-202","kind":"comment","references":"906f60a9683271f49db952dd9cd9c191ae0783d643686a88ca615a74350b1fcc","summary":"Resolution: In diff.rs, parse --fail-on and --kind once. For each listed kind that is neither a built-in Kind nor present on any record on either side, print to stderr `qualifier diff: warning: kind 'X' matches no known kind`. Apply the same check to threads --kind (threads.rs:110-114). This keeps custom kinds legal.","tags":["session:claude-code:906ddd45-2dc1-4a64-9674-56eec33bd6f1"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"src/cli/commands/diff.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-01T20:33:23.212509Z","id":"1d9e730585cba9486fe6227ca4982d417eba30bbcc292888b4ee7e6f8efc5fbb","body":{"detail":"Evidence: src/cli/commands/diff.rs:325-327 (`let Some(blob_oid) = qual_blobs_at_ref.get(&rel) else { continue; }` discards every union-only path)","kind":"comment","references":"3ec580af1e3489c5d00ca7afe51436cafb67e91b3f335835ade41b7754f79921","summary":"Resolution: In load_records_at_ref (diff.rs:303-329), iterate qual_blobs_at_ref directly and delete the union of new_qual_files paths. Drop the new_qual_files parameter and its argument at diff.rs:161. Reword the doc comment to say every .qual blob at <ref> is read, which is why deleted files still appear under Resolved.","tags":["session:claude-code:906ddd45-2dc1-4a64-9674-56eec33bd6f1"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"src/cli/commands/diff.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-01T20:33:23.212517Z","id":"964a292f32e53d11d46bd32278907660c953cf84b2989c42bcb02e56669185f0","body":{"detail":"Evidence: src/cli/commands/diff.rs:380-399 (Recorder::default + breadthfirst over the whole tree, then filters by is_qual_path)","kind":"comment","references":"33421b18a81c5b13900ec3bce2d68d57546cf9723edf42aeb4c8f50ef91a656f","summary":"Resolution: This is unmeasured. First time `qualifier diff <ref>` on a large tree (100k+ files). If it is slow, replace the full Recorder traversal at diff.rs:380-383 with a custom gix tree Visit delegate that records only .qual blobs and skips subtrees matched by .qualignore. That also fixes the ignore asymmetry in 6591fa81.","tags":["session:claude-code:906ddd45-2dc1-4a64-9674-56eec33bd6f1"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"src/cli/commands/diff.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-01T20:33:23.212524Z","id":"35829a856cbfa523f5ed3e307f742d2b796606b3783aa57b1ceeec664dad6fe0","body":{"detail":"Evidence: src/cli/commands/diff.rs:430-433 (`HashMap<&str, &Record>` collected from filter_map, last writer wins)","kind":"comment","references":"4cf7a6d3d7986f0b33c10000eb8e45927a60cb7ef97b211a2ff7d59878ffa1ad","summary":"Resolution: Change supersedes_index (diff.rs:430-433) to HashMap<&str, Vec<&Record>>, make ResolvedEntry.closer a Vec, and print one 'superseded/resolved by' continuation per closer in print_resolved. The CLI already refuses to supersede a non-live target (targets.rs:373), so duplicates arise only from merged branches, emit, or hand edits.","tags":["session:claude-code:906ddd45-2dc1-4a64-9674-56eec33bd6f1"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"src/cli/commands/diff.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-01T20:33:23.212533Z","id":"b1958bb513de6269e67675a0c72d1f082376a4439b17e317969f8f6a1ad1a785","body":{"detail":"Evidence: src/cli/commands/diff.rs:671-677 (only std::env::var(\"COLUMNS\"), else 80); `grep -n terminal_size Cargo.toml` finds no hits","kind":"comment","references":"3d50a1caf30fa746a15f2002c10be5b1ee4c8e8675a2f9acca77c8ebf54d3c53","summary":"Resolution: In term_width (diff.rs:671-677), keep $COLUMNS as an override, then fall back to the real terminal size via the terminal_size crate (it is not yet in Cargo.toml) when stdout is a TTY, then 80. Fix the doc comment at diff.rs:669-670, which claims shells set COLUMNS in the environment.","tags":["session:claude-code:906ddd45-2dc1-4a64-9674-56eec33bd6f1"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"src/cli/commands/freshness.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-01T20:33:23.212541Z","id":"a52bbc6e4ef86da65ce2b91c99efe17c743730daae8e0634d3ee060b9d20be6a","body":{"detail":"Evidence: src/cli/commands/freshness.rs:63 (`record.subject() != subject_filter` against the raw CLI string) vs src/cli/commands/show.rs:40-41 (Locator-based resolution)","kind":"comment","references":"2d2e2c6180161011aef22554c25b001b51e32af9fd082dd2dc9fd47d6672da91","summary":"Resolution: In freshness::run, replace find_project_root + qual_file::discover (freshness.rs:30-33) with targets::Locator::from_cwd()? and targets::discover_project, resolve args.subject via locator.subject(). Match that subject and its descendants, as threads' location filter does, instead of the raw string compare at freshness.rs:61-64. Update the [subject] text in review.md.","tags":["session:claude-code:906ddd45-2dc1-4a64-9674-56eec33bd6f1"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"src/cli/commands/ls.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-01T20:33:23.212552Z","id":"170178f2287f3dd6a27e9a38683ef257e5c595d32c7c5cc7707a416e261f76db","body":{"detail":"Evidence: src/cli/commands/ls.rs:31 (iterates every qf.records entry with no superseded filter) and ls.rs:79 (prints kinds.len() regardless of --kind)","kind":"comment","references":"2ee5791b02b2ca3aa1647983ac4c19645065f6a4124edf9fc5a1be35893ab1c4","summary":"Resolution: In ls.rs:29-41, collect records and run compact::filter_superseded first. Skip resolve-kind records, as show does at show.rs:63. When --kind is set, count only matching records in the human '(N annotations)' (ls.rs:79) and in JSON annotation_count (ls.rs:65).","tags":["session:claude-code:906ddd45-2dc1-4a64-9674-56eec33bd6f1"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"src/cli/commands/praise.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-01T20:33:23.212563Z","id":"928894aea6609ce747633b0e765397d839e7774a466b4cfca26348c731306a7a","body":{"detail":"Evidence: src/cli/commands/praise.rs:50 (only filter_superseded) and praise.rs:96 (prints att.issuer verbatim) vs src/cli/commands/agents/pages/praise.md:58-63","kind":"comment","references":"eabd4b164b88cdcdcc3d57c3547f291cd434b56d12c6ecfde2e84741ce1a29d6","summary":"Resolution: Make praise match praise.md. After filter_superseded at praise.rs:50, drop Kind::Resolve records and the replies of closed threads, sharing one helper with show (see 4a180e50, built on threads::build_threads). Move show's mailto shortening (show.rs:156-160) into a shared function and use it at praise.rs:96 for human output.","tags":["session:claude-code:906ddd45-2dc1-4a64-9674-56eec33bd6f1"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"src/cli/commands/show.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-01T20:33:23.212572Z","id":"fc153d6c1af52ddec6118e0023ee04008b501fab134b8cf21a87dbe7e50f1e15","body":{"detail":"Evidence: src/cli/commands/show.rs:117-125 (a reply whose parent was filtered out is pushed to roots), while show.rs:59-64 hides only superseded records and resolve records","kind":"comment","references":"4a180e508095189d3a35f0720728a70838a5b29fdcd45042cc6be2d878e845e7","summary":"Resolution: Rebuild show's view from threads::build_threads (src/threads.rs:55) filtered to the subject. By default print open threads only (root plus live replies). Under --all, print closed threads marked '(closed)' with their resolve. Delete the hand-rolled references tree at show.rs:107-127.","tags":["session:claude-code:906ddd45-2dc1-4a64-9674-56eec33bd6f1"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"src/cli/commands/show.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-01T20:33:23.212580Z","id":"7fa142310e7fd3e584ace89be972f580cd199c857efcc862ac29b5e8d8c10c6f","body":{"detail":"Evidence: src/cli/commands/show.rs:196 still cites `qualifier graph`; src/graph.rs does not exist and src/cli/mod.rs has no Graph variant","kind":"comment","references":"c9122713daace58fa2abae5b7e7f0f90fe6947fcf0f89fffb90037995715e083","summary":"Resolution: Change the comment at src/cli/commands/show.rs:196 to 'They remain visible in `--format json`.' (`qualifier graph` was removed).","tags":["session:claude-code:906ddd45-2dc1-4a64-9674-56eec33bd6f1"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"src/cli/commands/compact.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-01T20:33:23.212723Z","id":"961ba4bc2935346d154047d5e1587402495d00271e305f0f73b0aa8bfa1a6786","body":{"kind":"resolve","summary":"Duplicate of 03f495adbb90e75950573cf37150dcb05b1fb5c5f89e7e078f2d8cafd590d7a7, which makes the same claim with the same root cause; follow that thread.","supersedes":"2c70122de52aaa031c3ed16cc6fe79289d498e0665548b4e9b0a5c7ce2e8d46c","tags":["reason:duplicate","session:claude-code:906ddd45-2dc1-4a64-9674-56eec33bd6f1"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"src/cli/commands/diff.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-01T20:34:02.796904Z","id":"38119ecbfb1fdc63aed4d7b6f4435c4ac10b1df1dc1bb2d0c2568e8780097eae","body":{"detail":"Today it appears as Added and Resolved, and --fail-on trips on it (src/cli/commands/diff.rs:419-424, 276-288). Option A: add a Changed bucket keyed by thread origin (crate::threads) to human and JSON output, excluded from --fail-on unless the kind escalated. Option B: keep the output, exclude re-anchors from --fail-on only. Option C: keep current behavior and document that re-anchoring a blocker trips --fail-on. Recommendation: A; thread origins already exist, and re-anchoring is routine after edits, so CI should not fail on it.","kind":"comment","references":"f9b62efa2db9a963b78c1b1b0a7c6b3f3cb81e881ec6e28af98a3997249d6cb1","summary":"Needs a decision: How should qualifier diff classify a branch record that supersedes a record already present at <ref>?","tags":["status:needs-decision","session:claude-code:906ddd45-2dc1-4a64-9674-56eec33bd6f1"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"src/cli/commands/ls.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-01T20:34:02.796916Z","id":"46783114a30d7adf543ea2410235691224bbe29b8ebfa670a61c4b2e7b082f58","body":{"detail":"Option A: remove the flag (minor bump) and point to 'git ls-files' plus 'qualifier ls'. Option B: implement it by walking the project with ignore::Walk and listing files with no subject, after deciding which files count as artifacts. Option C: keep it but exit non-zero with 'not implemented'. Recommendation: A; a flag that silently returns nothing misleads, and B needs a definition of 'artifact' the project hasn't settled.","kind":"comment","references":"da1fabb901f1ff8a287213848ab2415caefb01ac1b65c936b821fa07f665a6ef","summary":"Needs a decision: What should happen to ls --unqualified, which is documented but always returns empty?","tags":["status:needs-decision","session:claude-code:906ddd45-2dc1-4a64-9674-56eec33bd6f1"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"src/cli/commands/show.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-01T20:34:02.796926Z","id":"a88f849fe3a1a21781b90fb92073e1ea9486d3beceacc1c9f77755ec9dbcd335","body":{"detail":"Option A: keep the current behavior and document the difference in ls.md; show.md already tells scripts to test the exit code. Option B: make show and praise exit 0 with an informational message (or [] in JSON); breaking for scripts, minor bump. Option C: a distinct exit code for 'no records'. Recommendation: A; the behavior is deliberate and documented, so documenting ls's difference is the cheapest consistent fix.","kind":"comment","references":"d02772f915399edb55722a27d5a55ff6a62d4ea4ce6176cb621465edc3f391e6","summary":"Needs a decision: Is an empty result an error? show and praise exit 1 when a subject has no records; ls exits 0.","tags":["status:needs-decision","session:claude-code:906ddd45-2dc1-4a64-9674-56eec33bd6f1"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"src/cli/commands/diff.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"human","created_at":"2026-10-01T20:44:46.624865Z","id":"2b3ab237cf8f951299e255f41f6df31028c9321b7e6af56d2857e7e161d6f32b","body":{"detail":"Add a Changed group","kind":"comment","references":"f9b62efa2db9a963b78c1b1b0a7c6b3f3cb81e881ec6e28af98a3997249d6cb1","summary":"Decided: add a Changed group to qualifier diff. Match branch records to records at <ref> by thread origin (crate::threads) and report edits and re-anchors under Changed in human and JSON output instead of Added + Resolved. --fail-on ignores Changed entries unless the kind became more severe (e.g. concern -> blocker).","tags":["status:decided","session:claude-code:906ddd45-2dc1-4a64-9674-56eec33bd6f1"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"src/cli/commands/ls.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"human","created_at":"2026-10-01T20:45:26.349848Z","id":"a47054e63e4b45c4bd8e832c643859d57658ebfaf7971908d4b3bc01895a42ec","body":{"detail":"Remove it","kind":"comment","references":"da1fabb901f1ff8a287213848ab2415caefb01ac1b65c936b821fa07f665a6ef","summary":"Decided: remove ls --unqualified (minor version bump). Drop the flag and its docs; point users at git ls-files together with qualifier ls.","tags":["status:decided","session:claude-code:906ddd45-2dc1-4a64-9674-56eec33bd6f1"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"src/cli/commands/show.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"human","created_at":"2026-10-01T20:46:21.935027Z","id":"b20b2592bae15ac3b6ab0ef9da034e8854b4477dbb972b8482623777ed1cc5f7","body":{"detail":"Make empty results succeed.  Then it's consistent with expectation.  You've shown that there are no things.","kind":"comment","references":"d02772f915399edb55722a27d5a55ff6a62d4ea4ce6176cb621465edc3f391e6","summary":"Decided: empty results succeed. show and praise exit 0 when a subject has no records, printing an informational message in human output and [] in JSON, consistent with ls. Breaking for scripts that test exit 1: minor version bump; update show.md:70-71 and praise.md.","tags":["status:decided","session:claude-code:906ddd45-2dc1-4a64-9674-56eec33bd6f1"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"src/cli/commands/record.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-01T20:47:33.143334Z","id":"3f9b5d0cf1a52ae94d3b4f2de39b6c667e4f5f06c5cd05bd39e2a9dbe35ef0e9","body":{"detail":"The write loop appends each planned record and then prints its result line (targets::append, then emit_batch_line). println! panics on EPIPE, because Rust ignores SIGPIPE by default. So `qualifier record --stdin < batch.jsonl | head -1` appended 2 of 59 planned records, then panicked with 'failed printing to stdout: Broken pipe (os error 32)'. The comment at line 315 says the batch is all-or-nothing without --continue-on-error. That holds for validation errors but not for output errors: a closed pipe leaves a partial batch, and rerunning the same file duplicates the records that were already written.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":319},"end":{"line":328},"content_hash":"ab233a26327b223e3eef57cbf1431d2a5137ef4a93c2b1eff2330edced9145ff"},"suggested_fix":"Make output failures unable to interrupt writes: append every planned record first, then print the result lines. Write the output with writeln! to a locked stdout and treat io::ErrorKind::BrokenPipe as a clean exit, not a panic. Add a CLI test that pipes a multi-line batch into a reader that closes after one line, then asserts every record was written and there was no panic.","summary":"record --stdin writes part of a batch and panics when stdout closes early","tags":["session:claude-code:906ddd45-2dc1-4a64-9674-56eec33bd6f1"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"src/cli/commands/threads.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-01T20:47:33.143422Z","id":"e9ce68bceb4a1c66ac0ed69c2d77ff7ca80287ee9ca6bce2102fac68d20ffe0c","body":{"detail":"In one run, `qualifier threads <19 full root IDs> --format json` (0.8.0, pinned plugin install) returned 22 open threads, none of them among the 19 requested; they were the root IDs of a separate set of threads in the same repository. Running the identical command again returned exactly the 19 requested threads. The 22 returned matched a set that another process in the same session was querying concurrently, so it may be a harness or shell mix-up, not qualifier. This record exists so a second occurrence can be correlated; ID arguments are parsed into LocationFilter here.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":104},"end":{"line":135},"content_hash":"6721c71ddc934e9c248d8f7c494dd4fd17949c5676775956319f90a6a954e42e"},"suggested_fix":"If it recurs, capture the exact argv, cwd and output. Add a CLI test that passes several full IDs in one call next to unrelated threads and asserts exactly those threads are returned. Close as invalid if a harness-side cause is found.","summary":"threads with full record IDs as arguments once returned a different set of threads (observed once, not reproduced)","tags":["session:claude-code:906ddd45-2dc1-4a64-9674-56eec33bd6f1"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"src/cli/commands/show.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-01T20:55:31.262750Z","id":"487a3bf1650733ea8feee240d0ae160aa858dc8cc08514c47b2abb4046644ff0","body":{"kind":"resolve","summary":"Duplicate of ee707fae87763ba8e2fe92a1da4d272638050e3dc27a1d83daaef614a15d47ed (src/threads.rs), which fixes this as part of one shared thread-state model and renderer for every read command.","supersedes":"4a180e508095189d3a35f0720728a70838a5b29fdcd45042cc6be2d878e845e7","tags":["reason:duplicate","session:claude-code:906ddd45-2dc1-4a64-9674-56eec33bd6f1"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"src/cli/commands/praise.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-01T20:55:31.262946Z","id":"c903c89e30e83489f361042c24d9d25a17f841861c374e177ac45828b1a61c95","body":{"kind":"resolve","summary":"Duplicate of ee707fae87763ba8e2fe92a1da4d272638050e3dc27a1d83daaef614a15d47ed (src/threads.rs), which fixes this as part of one shared thread-state model and renderer for every read command.","supersedes":"eabd4b164b88cdcdcc3d57c3547f291cd434b56d12c6ecfde2e84741ce1a29d6","tags":["reason:duplicate","session:claude-code:906ddd45-2dc1-4a64-9674-56eec33bd6f1"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"src/cli/commands/emit.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-01T21:51:04.686192Z","id":"2e136892b1165356d20ed0e63bd30f61131704d5f921215d66692d884a790250","body":{"kind":"comment","references":"d7b8f76adcbb216d79c38355947ea09f8936f77b8c71601d5b88382b98454962","summary":"Depends on 12542525: the ID for Unknown records is hashed over the envelope-ordered view 12542525 introduces","tags":["depends-on:12542525d52f83be6475df6bc3d6fb7e2aca3740a603987f1ba741fe2e4003c5","session:claude-code:906ddd45-2dc1-4a64-9674-56eec33bd6f1"]}}
